"""Original concise practice lessons. Educational content is separate from live diagnosis."""
SCHOOL='https://www.iracing.com/iracing-101-the-iracing-driving-school/'
RULES='https://www.iracing.com/iracing-official-sporting-code/'
LESSONS=[
('Vision & reference points','Foundations','Look through the corner toward where the car should go. Use repeatable trackside objects for braking, turn-in and exit; shadows and movable cones can change. Keep enough awareness nearby to react to traffic.','Practice: choose three fixed visual markers for one corner. Run five consistent laps before moving the braking point.','Review: braking-point spread and clean-lap consistency.',SCHOOL),
('Racing line & late apex','Foundations','The useful line depends on the next straight, car, grip and traffic. An exit leading onto a long straight often rewards a later apex and earlier straightening. A geometric shortest path is not automatically quickest.','Practice: compare two lines at the same entry speed. Judge the full corner and following straight, not minimum speed alone.','Review: corner time, throttle pickup and speed at the next fixed marker.',SCHOOL),
('Threshold braking','Braking','Build brake input decisively while the car is straight, then reduce input as speed and available grip change. Maximum pedal input is not always maximum deceleration. ABS behavior varies by car and setting.','Practice: use a fixed braking point and increase peak input in small steps during solo practice. Compare repeatability before moving the marker.','Review: approach speed, peak brake input and braking distance. Pedal percentage is not measured pressure.',SCHOOL),
('Trail braking & release','Braking','As steering demand rises, progressively release brake demand. The useful amount and release shape depend on the car and corner; carrying brake deeper is not automatically faster. Abrupt release can disturb balance.','Practice: hold entry speed constant and make the last part of the release smoother. Avoid combining a later marker with more trail brake on the same test lap.','Review: brake trace, steering build-up, minimum speed and exit time.',SCHOOL),
('Red string / steering-pedal analogy','Foundations','Imagine a string linking the steering wheel to your pedals: more steering usually means less spare grip for hard braking or acceleration. As you unwind the wheel, you can often feed in more throttle. This is a teaching analogy, not a literal mechanical relationship or universal formula.','Practice: on a slow corner, deliberately unwind the wheel smoothly while progressively adding throttle. Adapt to available grip.','Review: overlap of steering, brake release and throttle pickup; watch for corrections.',SCHOOL),
('Weight transfer & load sensitivity','Car balance','Braking shifts load toward the front; acceleration shifts it rearward; cornering shifts it toward the outside. These changes affect available tire forces. Total grip does not simply rise with load, and sudden inputs can upset balance.','Practice: compare abrupt and progressive transitions at modest speed. Keep the line fixed and feel how the car settles.','Review: longitudinal/lateral acceleration and input smoothness. The app does not measure each tire’s live contact load.',SCHOOL),
('Steering: more is not always better','Car balance','Steer enough to follow the intended path. Adding lock after the front tires have saturated may scrub speed without improving rotation. Too little angle can also reflect excessive entry speed or an intentionally wider line. Wheel angle alone does not diagnose either condition.','Practice: at a fixed entry speed, compare steering build-up and line with a matched reference. Confirm steering ratio and calibration first.','Review: steering-wheel angle at the same distance and speed, together with GPS line and corrections.',SCHOOL),
('Understeer & oversteer','Car balance','Understeer means the car turns less than requested; oversteer means the rear rotates more than intended. Causes can include speed, input timing, tires, aero and setup. Avoid treating every balance problem as a setup problem.','Practice: reduce entry speed slightly and repeat the same line before changing the garage. Separate entry, middle and exit symptoms.','Review: line, yaw response and steering corrections. This app’s angle comparison is not a tire-slip measurement.',SCHOOL),
('Throttle pickup & exit','Corner exits','Early throttle helps only when the car can use it. A brief early application followed by a lift can cost more than a slightly later, sustained application. Prioritize a line that lets you unwind steering and accelerate.','Practice: aim for one smooth throttle build rather than repeated stabs. Compare speed at a fixed point down the following straight.','Review: sustained throttle pickup distance, lifts and exit sector time.',SCHOOL),
('Drafting / slipstream','Racecraft','Following a car can reduce aerodynamic drag and increase closing speed. In aero-sensitive cars, disturbed air may reduce cornering performance. The tow can bring you to the braking zone faster, so your solo-lap marker may be unsuitable.','Practice: in a controlled session, maintain room to react and compare approach speed with and without a tow. Plan an escape before committing to a pass.','Review: approach speed and observed gap trend. Gaps here are passage-time estimates.',SCHOOL),
('Controlled late braking vs dive-bombing','Racecraft','A late pass needs control, a realistic path through the corner and room for both cars. A desperate lunge that relies on the other driver avoiding contact is poor racecraft. Another car’s presence can change the line and stopping space you need.','Practice: rehearse side-by-side braking with a willing partner. Start with ample margin; abandon attempts you cannot complete under control.','Review: replay, overlap, closing speed and racing room. Consult the current Sporting Code for rules.',RULES),
('Overtaking & setting up a pass','Racecraft','Build a pass from the previous exit. Identify where your relative speed advantage is useful, show intent predictably and leave room. Winning the entry but losing the exit can hand the position back.','Practice: compare an inside line with a normal line over the whole sequence. Use a partner or AI practice before racing.','Review: exit speed, side-by-side control and the next corner’s position.',SCHOOL),
('Defending without reactive blocking','Racecraft','Choose a defensive position predictably and early. Do not assume rules from another racing series apply in iRacing. Reactively moving to impede a pursuing car can constitute blocking under the Sporting Code.','Practice: select the defensive line before the braking phase and leave a usable path for an alongside car.','Review: current Sporting Code and replay. The app does not issue stewarding verdicts.',RULES),
('Switchback / crossover','Racecraft','If a rival compromises the apex or exit, a patient turn-in can let you straighten earlier and accelerate past. It works through timing and space, not by aiming across an occupied lane.','Practice: allow a partner to take the inside, delay your turn slightly and focus on exit traction. Stay ready to lift if paths converge.','Review: minimum speed, steering unwind and relative exit speed.',SCHOOL),
('Multiclass traffic','Racecraft','Closing speeds can be large. Predictability and clear intent matter more than chasing a tiny time gain. Faster cars should plan passes; slower cars should avoid sudden movements that make intentions hard to read.','Practice: identify safe passing windows in advance and rehearse lifting earlier if a corner will be occupied.','Review: replay, class speed difference and time spent in traffic; check event-specific rules.',RULES),
('Wet driving & changing grip','Conditions','Standing water, damp patches and painted surfaces can change the grip available. A dry reference can become unsuitable. Use gentler transitions and reassess braking margins as conditions evolve.','Practice: build pace progressively with a reference from comparable conditions. Avoid chasing dry braking markers in the wet.','Review: wetness, temperature, reference context and input repeatability.',SCHOOL),
('Tire management','Conditions','Sliding, sustained scrubbing and wheelspin can hurt tire performance. A fast isolated lap is not always the best race pace. Tire pressure and temperature information in the simulator may be delayed or limited.','Practice: compare two equal-length clean runs with consistent fuel and conditions. Judge average pace and degradation.','Review: stint lap trend and available tire data. Missing live temperatures are not a cold-tire measurement.',SCHOOL),
('Starts & first-lap judgment','Racecraft','Cold equipment, traffic and uncertain grip make the opening lap different from a solo hotlap. Leave reaction room and expect earlier braking in a queue. A clean opening often matters more than one position.','Practice: rehearse starts in AI races and identify escape routes before braking.','Review: incidents, overlap and braking consistency; obey the event’s start procedures.',RULES),
('Safe rejoining & incident recovery','Racecraft','After an off-track or spin, regain control and check approaching traffic before rejoining. An immediate return to the racing line can create a second incident. Stay predictable and avoid sudden reversals.','Practice: use replay and mirrors to learn where approaching cars become visible. Rehearse recovery in solo practice.','Review: visibility, traffic position and current Sporting Code.',RULES),
('Fuel, stint pace & consistency','Race preparation','Plan for the session distance, consumption and a practical margin. Consistent pace with fewer mistakes can beat isolated fast laps. Traffic and conditions affect consumption and achievable lap time.','Practice: record a representative run rather than extrapolating from one lap. Use comparable starting fuel for setup tests.','Review: lap spread, fuel usage and clean-lap average. This app does not guarantee a race strategy.',SCHOOL),
('Setup experiments','Race preparation','Change one adjustable item at a time. Keep weather, fuel, tires and driving approach comparable. Fixed setups and car-specific adjustment limits matter. A quicker lap after a change is not proof that the change caused it.','Practice: use the Garage baseline/test workflow, then repeat the baseline to check learning and changing conditions.','Review: several clean laps, average pace, spread and the reported entry/mid/exit symptom.',SCHOOL),
('Deliberate practice & debrief','Foundations','Pick one repeatable weakness. Separate observation, hypothesis and experiment: early braking is an observation; fear of entry speed is only a possible explanation. Keep useful feedback small enough to apply next lap.','Practice: select one Training filter, run several clean laps, review the ranked losses and retain only changes that repeat.','Review: consistency and the whole corner’s time, not a single attractive trace.',SCHOOL)
]
